{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_13-51-31-omnipaxos-real-run/agent-source && head -40 docs/omnipaxos/compaction.md | cat -n | sed -n '1,40p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tAs time passes, the replicated log in `OmniPaxos` will grow large. To avoid letting the log growing infinitely large, we support two ways of compaction that can be initiated by users:\n     2\t\n     3\t## Trim\n     4\tTrimming the log removes all entries up to a certain index. Since the entries are deleted from the log, a trim operation can only be done if **ALL** nodes in the cluster have decided up to that index. Example:\n     5\t\n     6\t```rust\n     7\tuse omnipaxos::CompactionErr;\n     8\t\n     9\t// we will try trimming the first 100 entries of the log.\n    10\tlet trim_idx = Some(100);  // using `None` will use the highest trimmable index\n    11\tmatch omni_paxos.trim(trim_idx) {\n    12\t    Ok(_) => {\n    13\t        // later, we can see that the trim succeeded with `omni_paxos.get_compacted_idx()`\n    14\t    }\n    15\t    Err(e) => {\n    16\t        match e {\n    17\t            CompactionErr::NotAllDecided(idx) => {\n    18\t                // Our provided trim index was not decided by all servers yet. All servers have currently only decided up to `idx`.\n    19\t                // If desired, users can retry with omni_paxos.trim(Some(idx)) which will then succeed.\n    20\t            }\n    21\t            _ => {}\n    22\t        }\n    23\t    }\n    24\t}\n    25\t``` \n    26\t\n    27\t> **Note:** Make sure your application really does not need the data that will be trimmed anymore. Once it is succeeded, the trimmed entries are lost and cannot be read or recovered.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Snapshot\n    30\tTrimming compacts the log and discards any data preceding the trim index. For safety, it therefore requires all servers to have decided the trim index. If you don't want to discard any data and the entries in the log are such that they can be compacted into a snapshot, `OmniPaxos` supports snapshotting decided entries of the log. For instance, in our kv-store example, we don't need to keep every log entry that changes the kv-pairs. Instead, if we want to snapshot the log, it is sufficient to keep the latest value for every key. We implement our snapshot as a struct called `KVSnapshot` which is just a wrapper for a `HashMap` that will hold the latest value for every key in the log. To make it work with `OmniPaxos`, we need to implement the trait `Snapshot` for `KVSnapshot`:\n    31\t\n    32\t```rust\n    33\tuse std::collections::HashMap;\n    34\tuse omnipaxos::storage::Snapshot;\n    35\t\n    36\t#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\n    37\tpub struct KVSnapshot {\n    38\t    snapshotted: HashMap<String, u64>\n    39\t}\n    40\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
